Implementation review correction batch for EXTENSIONS_UI_PLAN.md.

Backend: explicit null serialization prevents stale UI fields from being resurrected by tolerant registry merges; invalid legacy UI strings are dropped during migration; malformed launch arguments resolve to safe mode; load-stage advancement and abort now require the active CSPRNG acknowledgement token; marker discard updates in-memory state; UTF-8 prefix checks use strip_prefix.

Frontend: panel slots now live in shared components/ui and a lint boundary prevents extension-ui production code from importing consumer domains. Standard mount failures are isolated and observed failures clear only their own journal. Advanced mounts defer while an anchor is absent, remain unavailable in Chat, clean up on unmount, and remount when the allowed surface returns. Action results use a body portal, open upward from the composer, scroll, and close by button, Escape, or outside click. Dialog focus is trapped and restored. Theme IDs follow extension:<extension-id>:<theme-id>. Catalog limits, unknown projections, and bounded catch-up retries are enforced.

Host and contract: action results are materialized before validation and remeasured after canonicalization; canonical IDs are revalidated after prefixing; standard UI accepts the explicit null entry emitted by Rust. A generated maxInFlightActionsPerExtension=8 authority prevents one extension from exhausting a shared host. Exact CSP, exact-size protocol input, malformed theme values, prototype-shaped keys, getter results, cross-extension admission, and post-normalization growth are covered. Dead SVG/bin loaders were removed and the generated release helper is ignored.

Design choices: B-04 uses deferred mounting instead of making the third-party composer anchor active in Chat, preserving the product rule that Chat exposes no plugin UI. The broad per-record registry quarantine mentioned as a secondary recommendation was not folded into this correction: A-01 and A-06 are closed at their sources, while generic quarantine needs its own versioned raw-record preservation design to avoid silently losing user data.

